---
import Docs from "../layouts/Docs.astro";
import DocSection from "../components/docs/DocSection.astro";
import CommandBlock from "../components/docs/CommandBlock.astro";
import Callout from "../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import { block, slashCommands } from "../components/docs/commands";

// One list drives both the table of contents and the section headings.
const S = {
  how: { id: "how-it-works", title: "How a session works" },
  ingest: { id: "ingest", title: "1. Ingest your resume, GitHub and LinkedIn" },
  review: { id: "review", title: "2. Review what ARTie learned" },
  tailor: { id: "tailor", title: "3. Tailor to a job posting" },
  approve: { id: "approve", title: "4. Approve bullets and set preferences" },
  render: { id: "render", title: "5. Render the PDF" },
  ui: { id: "editor", title: "6. Edit by hand in artie ui (optional)" },
  later: { id: "later", title: "Everything else you can run" },
};
const toc = Object.values(S);
---

<Docs
  title="Usage: ARTie"
  description="A first session with ARTie: add your resume, GitHub projects and LinkedIn, tailor to a job posting, approve bullets, set preferences, and render a PDF."
  heading="Usage"
  lede="A first session, start to finish. Install ARTie first if you haven't."
  toc={toc}
>
  <DocSection section={S.how}>
    <p>
      You talk to your coding agent, as you always do. ARTie gives it a memory of your real
      experience and a set of checks. The agent reads and writes; ARTie keeps your facts, your
      preferences and every version, and checks each change before it is saved. <strong>Every
      fact on your resume must come from something you gave it.</strong> If the agent needs one
      that isn't there, it asks you.
    </p>
    <p>
      In Claude Code you run <code>/artie:*</code> commands. Codex has two skills instead, <code>$artie-setup</code> and <code>$artie-tailor</code>,
      and for everything else you ask in plain words. This page shows the Claude Code command
      first, then the Codex equivalent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where a step has no command of its own, the page says what to ask your agent and names the
      ARTie tool it will call, in case you want to see what happened.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.ingest}>
    <p>Gather three kinds of source. You don't need all of them:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your current resume as a PDF, DOCX, Markdown or LaTeX file.</li>
      <li>
        Your LinkedIn data export (the archive you request from LinkedIn's own settings).
      </li>
      <li>
        Your GitHub repositories. Sign in once (<a href="/install#github">the Install page</a>
        shows how), then ask your agent to list your repositories
        (<code>list_github_repos</code>) and pick the ones that matter. It reads them, or offers
        to clone them. Or skip the login: clone them yourself and hand over the folders.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Then run the setup command with the paths:</p>
    <CommandBlock label="In Claude Code (replace the placeholder with your paths)" lines={block("artie-setup")} />
    <p>
      In Codex, run <code>$artie-setup</code> and tell it where the files are. If you give no
      paths, the agent asks.
    </p>
    <p>What happens next:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The agent reads your files itself (ARTie never opens them) and fills ARTie's record
        formats with <code>ingest_schema</code>, then stores them with <code>upsert_items</code>.
        Titles, companies, dates and bullets are kept verbatim. For a repository, it describes
        what the code shows and checks each claim against the files.
      </li>
      <li>
        Anything you edited by hand is never overwritten, and anything you deleted is not added
        back unless you ask.
      </li>
      <li>
        It asks where each project was done: for a job, for a degree, or on your own. Work
        counts as stronger evidence than coursework, so answer each one
        (<code>suggest_project_contexts</code>, then <code>set_project_context</code>).
      </li>
      <li>
        It sets your resume header (name, email, links) with <code>update_profile</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        If a fact depends on the posting, tell it. For example: "my graduation date is June
        2027, but December 2027 when a role needs me enrolled after the internship." ARTie then
        asks that question of every posting.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.review}>
    <p>
      The setup ends with a summary of what is stored. There is no separate command for
      reviewing, so ask your agent in plain words:
    </p>
    <CommandBlock
      label="Say this to your agent"
      lines={["Show me everything ARTie has stored about me, grouped by kind, and point out anything duplicated or thin."]}
    />
    <p>
      It will call <code>list_items</code> and, for details, <code>get_item</code> and <code>get_profile</code>.
      Look for wrong dates, duplicate entries, and projects with no short bullets of their own.
      To search, ask something like "what do I have that shows Python?"
      (<code>kg_search</code>).
    </p>
    <p>
      To fix a fact, tell the agent the correction. It re-sends that record to <code>upsert_items</code> marked
      as a correction. Nothing is invented to fill a gap: if the graph holds nothing for a
      requirement, the agent says so.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.tailor}>
    <p>Paste the posting, or give a link, to the tailor command:</p>
    <CommandBlock label="In Claude Code (replace the placeholder with the posting)" lines={block("artie-tailor")} />
    <p>In Codex, run <code>$artie-tailor</code> and paste the posting.</p>
    <p>The agent works through these steps, and you can watch each one:</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Reads your brief.</strong> <code>art_briefing</code> returns your pinned rules,
        your preferences, and any yes/no questions to ask about the posting (such as whether it
        requires enrollment after the internship). It answers them from the posting's own words,
        or asks you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Opens the job.</strong> <code>open_job</code> stores the posting and its
        requirements, and picks your saved starting point for that kind of role if you have one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reads your experience</strong> and asks <code>suggest_actions</code> which of
        your approved bullets fit this posting.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Shows you a plan and waits.</strong> The plan says which items lead, what each
        revised bullet will say, what is cut and why, and the skills line. <em>Nothing is
        changed until you approve it.</em>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Runs it.</strong> After you approve, <code>execute_plan</code> applies the plan.
        ARTie checks each change and refuses or reverts one that claims more than its source
        supports, mentions a topic you banned, or breaks a rule you set. The agent tells you
        about every one rather than quietly resubmitting it.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <Callout title="You are the editor">
      <p>
        Reply to the plan in plain words: "lead with the recommender project", "cut the second
        internship's last bullet", "approved." The agent revises the plan and shows it again.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.approve}>
    <h3>Bullets</h3>
    <p>
      When a bullet reads the way you want, ask your agent to keep it: "Add that bullet to my
      library." It becomes a draft (<code>promote_bullet</code>). It joins your approved library
      only when you say yes (<code>approve_variant</code>); the agent never approves one on its
      own. Next time, <code>suggest_actions</code> offers your approved wording first, and a
      bullet that strays too far from it is reverted.
    </p>
    <p>
      To make a whole version the starting point for a kind of role, say so and name the role,
      for example "save this as my data science baseline" (<code>save_baseline</code>). A new
      job for that kind of role then starts as a copy of it.
    </p>

    <h3>Preferences</h3>
    <p>
      State a lasting rule as you would to a person: "never mention Kafka", "always lead with
      Python". When a message looks like a lasting rule (ARTie's hook flags it in Claude Code;
      on a host without the hook, your agent checks it with <code>observe</code>) the agent asks
      you to confirm how it read it: what it is about, whether to feature it or leave it out,
      how firm, and whether it is for every resume or only this job. Only after you say yes does
      it save the rule (<code>record_preference</code>).
    </p>
    <p>
      A rule you call absolute ("never") becomes a pin: what it names can never reach the page,
      even in other words. ARTie never saves one without your confirmation. To see what ARTie
      holds, run <code>/artie:prefs</code> in Claude Code, or ask your agent to show your
      preferences (<code>art_briefing</code>; <code>art_pins</code> returns the pins word for
      word).
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.render}>
    <p>
      The tailor command ends by rendering, so the PDF comes with the plan you approved. To
      render again after a change, ask your agent to "render the resume" (the <code>render</code> tool).
      It writes <code>resume.tex</code> and <code>resume.pdf</code> to <code>~/.art/applications/&lt;Company&gt;_&lt;Role&gt;/</code>,
      and the agent tells you the path.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The page is one page. If the draft runs over, ARTie reports where to cut and the agent
        cuts and renders again. Nothing is trimmed silently.
      </li>
      <li>
        With no LaTeX engine installed, ARTie downloads a pinned Tectonic on the first render.
        Offline, you get the <code>.tex</code> file and a hint on how to get a PDF.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.ui}>
    <p>
      To adjust the layout or the LaTeX yourself, open the local editor. In Claude Code, run <code>/artie:open</code> and
      name the job. It prints the command below with the job's id filled in. Run it yourself in
      a terminal; the agent does not start it.
    </p>
    <CommandBlock label="In a terminal (replace the job id)" lines={block("art-ui")} />
    <p>
      The editor opens at <code>http://127.0.0.1:8765/</code> and runs only on your machine.
      Anything you save or drag there comes back to the agent on your next message, and it
      builds on your edits instead of overwriting them. In Codex, ask the agent for the <code>artie
      ui</code> command for your job.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.later}>
    <p>The Claude Code commands, all in one place:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {slashCommands.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{item.command}</code></td>
            <td>{item.what}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The two skills behind <code>/artie:setup</code> and <code>/artie:tailor</code> also start on
      their own when you ask your agent to tailor a resume or update your experience. In Codex,
      ask for history in plain words; the agent uses <code>history</code> and <code>diff_nodes</code> to
      show versions and <code>checkout</code> to go back, after it shows you the difference and
      you confirm.
    </p>
  </DocSection>
</Docs>
